"n":8},{"id":42147038712068,"type":"genre","name":"Country Pop","n":4},{"id":53681850040905,"type":"genre","name":"Rock & Roll","n":2},{"id":247802226836647,"type":"genre","name":"Brill Building","n":1},{"id":100106212412601,"type":"genre","name":"Teen Pop","n":1},{"id":32857844513644,"type":"genre","name":"Rockabilly","n":1}],"labels":[{"id":55581389852106,"type":"label","name":"Acuff Rose (publishing deal)","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":7,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"},{"id":56451395848014,"type":"label","name":"A.C.O.F.R.O.S. (Acuff-Rose Publishing)","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":1,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"}],"album_labels":[],"own_scenes":[],"scenes":[{"id":210461960545621,"name":"El soul conquista Gran Bretaña","book":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","start":1961,"end":1967,"desc":"El 26 de octubre «Reach Out I’ll Be There» de los Four Tops, con la flauta de aire oriental, el fraseo dylaniano de Lamont Dozier y la interpretación de su vida de Levi Stubbs, desbancó a «Distant Drums» y fue número 1: el momento en que la música negra americana ocupó el centro de la cultura juvenil británica, al revés que en el boom del beat. Lo habían preparado durante años los entusiastas: Dave Godin, que fundó en 1963 la Tamla Motown Appreciation Society, viajó a Detroit, llevó «Where Did Our Love Go» a los piratas y bautizó el sello Tamla Motown (marzo de 1965, con una gira de la revista que fracasó fuera de Londres); Guy Stevens, que pinchaba discos negros en el Scene y dirigió Sue Records para Chris Blackwell; Roger Eagle en el Twisted Wheel de Mánchester; Tony Hall promocionando Atlantic («In the Midnight Hour», el «My Girl» de Otis Redding); el DJ Jeff Dexter mezclando soul y ska en Tiles, el club subterráneo de Oxford Street del mod de masas que visitó Tom Wolfe. En otoño de 1966 Atlantic y Motown copaban las listas; Otis Redding metió a 8.000 personas en Tiles; los Four Tops arrasaron en el Savile de Brian Epstein el 13 de noviembre. El racismo, aun así, seguía ahí.","color":"#facc15","family":"Soul, Motown, R&B y ska","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/#soul-reino-unido"},{"id":169050477627223,"name":"Antes del diluvio","book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","start":1957,"end":1962,"desc":"La «edad oscura» del rock'n'roll: los rebeldes son sustituidos por ídolos adolescentes educados y el Brill Building devuelve el poder a los compositores profesionales. Pero en esos años se incuban la revolución futura: la guitarra fuzz de Link Wray, los bailes de moda (el twist), el rock instrumental de guitarras (Duane Eddy, Ventures, Shadows), el calipso y el primer ska, el doo-wop más inventivo (Coasters, Drifters, Five Royales), el nacimiento del soul (Sam Cooke, Ray Charles, Motown, Stax), el estudio como instrumento de Joe Meek y el surf de los Beach Boys y del «muro de sonido» de Phil Spector.","color":"#f59e0b","family":"Raíces: rock'n'roll, country y roots","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#before-flood"}],"book_tracks":[{"id":149714657562192,"artist":"Everly Brothers","artist_id":206553628925579,"title":"Bye Bye Love","year":1957,"ess":0,"yt":"LRyrWN-fftE","kind":"s","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Antes del diluvio","scene_id":169050477627223,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#before-flood","chapters":["Sección 2.3"],"pages":[[1,17,null]]},{"id":256855268867145,"artist":"Everly Brothers","artist_id":206553628925579,"title":"All I Have To Do Is Dream","year":1958,"ess":0,"yt":"tbU3zdAgiX8","kind":"s","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Antes del diluvio","scene_id":169050477627223,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#before-flood","chapters":["Sección 2.3"],"pages":[[1,17,null]]},{"id":253214386471139,"artist":"The Everly Brothers","artist_id":206553628925579,"title":"Cathy’s Clown","year":1960,"ess":0,"yt":"PXvKAWiU_cQ","kind":null,"book":{"id":1,"title":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/"},"scene":"El soul conquista Gran Bretaña","scene_id":210461960545621,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/#soul-reino-unido","chapters":["Noviembre: Good Vibrations: Motown y el soul en Reino Unido, los Beach Boys y el Sunset Strip"],"pages":[[1,322],[1,465]]},{"id":187077582081241,"artist":"The Everly Brothers","artist_id":206553628925579,"title":"How Can I Meet Her?","year":1962,"ess":0,"yt":"j8T1h22O44M","kind":null,"book":{"id":1,"title":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/"},"scene":"El soul conquista Gran Bretaña","scene_id":210461960545621,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/#soul-reino-unido","chapters":["Noviembre: Good Vibrations: Motown y el soul en Reino Unido, los Beach Boys y el Sunset Strip"],"pages":[[1,322],[1,465]]}],"mentions":[{"book":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","page":322,"text":"…day, along with the small amount of imports that we had, and what was getting released here that had that black influence. But, of course, the pops, the Everly Brothers, Carole King, were all very important in our grounding. “How Can I Meet Her?” or “Cathy’s Clown”. Or people like Dion, “The Wanderer”. That was an…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":17,"text":"…music to yield a more \"traditional\" (and white) form of music for young people, as was the case with Johnny Cash's I Walk The Line (1956), and the Everly Brothers' Bye Bye Love (1957) and All I Have To Do Is Dream (1958). In 1958 Don Kirshner opened offices for songwriters and producers at the Brill Building…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":194,"text":"BoDeans (1) coined a catchy rootsy style with Love And Hope And Sex And Dreams (1986), a style that ran the gamut from the Everly Brothers to Fleetwood Mac (as their 1991 hit Good Things would prove). EIEIO (1) were even more varied, evoking Byrds, Little Feat, Band and other masters of…"}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"\"Speed-Fueled\" Breakup (1973)","body":"The Everly Brothers, Don and Phil, experienced a significant fallout, leading to a \"speed-fueled\" breakup in 1973. Following this event, they spoke to each other only once in the subsequent 10 years, which occurred at their father's funeral. \"The Everly Brothers, Don and Phil, did not get along. After a speed-fueled breakup in 1973, they talked to each other just once in the following 10 years, and that was at their father's funeral.\"","year":1973,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Oasis at War Part 2 - Revisited","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5bB7GAUM63Y"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Speed-Fueled Breakup (1973)","body":"Don and Phil Everly had a significant falling out, leading to a \"speed-fueled breakup\" in 1973. Following this, they spoke to each other only once in the subsequent 10 years, which occurred at their father's funeral. \"After a speed-fueled breakup in 1973, they talked to each other just once in the following 10 years, and that was at their father's funeral.\"","year":1973,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Oasis At War: Part 2","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cjWxC2jQVrA"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Acuff Rose Publishing Arrangement","body":"The Everly Brothers had a deal with the publishing company Acuff Rose, stipulating they would only perform songs published by that company. This created a conflict when Roy Orbison, whose songs were published by Sam Phillips, pitched \"Claudette\" to them. \"They had a deal with the publishing company Acuff Rose that they would only perform songs that were published by that company, and Orbison had a contract with Sam Phillips that meant that Orbison's songs were all published by Phillips.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 42: \"Ooby Dooby\" by Roy Orbison and the Teen Kings","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1zvzpXqr-k8"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Cover of \"Love Is Strange\"","body":"The Eiffelate Brothers were among the many artists who covered Mickey & Sylvia's hit song \"Love Is Strange.\" \"The Eiffelate Brothers.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 49: \"Love is Strange\" by Mickey and Sylvia","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FkyLPVvPbcI"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Managed and Published by Wesley Rose","body":"The Everly Brothers were managed and published by Wesley Rose, who also managed and published Roy Orbison, facilitating backroom deals like Orbison placing his song \"Claudette\" with them. \"He was also the Everly Brothers' manager and publisher, which is how Orbison had managed to place Claudeette with them.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 83: \"Only the Lonely\" by Roy Orbison","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uDvkjXa1ALk"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Popular with British Teenagers","body":"British teenagers were listening to their American records, which contrasted with the British commercial rock and roll industry. Harry Webb (Cliff Richard) had a particular taste for smoother American soft rockers like them. \"British teenagers were instead listening to American records by Buddy Holly or Little Richard, the Avley Brothers or Elvis.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 70: \"Move It\" by Cliff Richard and the Drifters","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uoEWP94f6S4"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Rockabilly Classification Debate","body":"The Everly Brothers, appearing in 1957 as country-influenced white rock and rollers, are often termed rockabilly. However, enthusiasts of the genre frequently disregard this classification, asserting that their sound lacks the defining characteristics of music originating from Sun Studios. \"And those singers will often get referred to as rockabilly. They don't tend to get counted by aficionados of the subjumper, who think they don't sound enough like the music from sun to count.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 63: \"Susie Q\", by Dale Hawkins","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4vqj1DcXOfw"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Songwriters (Year not specified)","body":"Felice and Boudleaux Bryant wrote many hit songs for The Everly Brothers. \"Feliz and Woodlow's Bryant, who'd written many hits for his friends, the Evli Brothers.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 74: \"It Doesn't Matter Any More\" by Buddy Holly","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6unib5T2mDQ"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Turned Down \"Only the Lonely\"","body":"The Everly Brothers, who were friends with Roy Orbison and shared his manager Wesley Rose, were offered \"Only the Lonely\" but turned it down. This is attributed to the song's unusually bizarre structure for a pop hit. \"They also, though, tried to get it recorded by the Everly Brothers, who were friends of Orbison, were also signed with A.C.O.F.R.O.S. and were also merged by Wesley Rose, and even they turned it down.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 83: \"Only the Lonely\" by Roy Orbison","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uDvkjXa1ALk"}]}],"awards":[{"kind":"award","title":"Rock and Roll Hall of Fame (1986)","body":"Won.","year":1986,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null